 ## Background
-Pruning in 3D Gaussian Splatting emerged as a solution to the high memory consumption and computational cost associated with the large number of Gaussians required for high-fidelity reconstruction. Early methods relied on simple opacity thresholds, but later approaches introduced geometric constraints (vertex pruning for mixed primitives) and optimization-based selection (SplatQ, learnable masks) to maintain quality at lower densities. Visibility-based pruning leverages the fact that occluded Gaussians contribute little to the final image, allowing for aggressive reduction in complex scenes.
+Pruning in 3D Gaussian Splatting emerged as a solution to the high memory consumption and computational cost associated with the large number of Gaussians required for high-fidelity reconstruction [G]. Early methods relied on simple opacity thresholds, but later approaches introduced geometric constraints (vertex pruning for mixed primitives) and optimization-based selection (SplatQ, learnable masks) to maintain quality at lower densities [G]. Visibility-based pruning leverages the fact that occluded Gaussians contribute little to the final image, allowing for aggressive reduction in complex scenes [S2].
 
